  "query_preview": "..(Thursday through Tuesday)\nIssued at 158 PM EDT TUE OCT 20 2026\n\nTranquil weather looks to continue through the long term period, \nand with a fairly stagnant pattern in place, weather will change \nvery little from day to day. Aloft, a closed low will initially be\nlocated off the coast of the Northeast, with midlevel troughing \nover the eastern US; meanwhile, a strong and expansive ridge will \nsit over the central CONUS. Over the course of the period, this \ntrough will gradually break down, allowing the ridge to expand \neastward closer to our area, though eastern Kentucky still looks \nto be under the influence of ridging through early next week. \nThus, at the surface, models are in good agreement that high \npressure will dominate, keeping weather uneventful through the \nforecast period. With such a dry air mass in place, even clouds \nwill be hard to come by through Saturday. Thus, expect plenty of \nsunshine and high temperatures near average in the upper 60s and \nlow 70s. Each night, clear skies and a weak pressure gradient will\nfavor the development of ridge valley temperatures splits, \naccompanied by typical valley fog. MOS guidance suggests that lows\nwill fall into the upper 30s in sheltered valleys and the low 40s\nin most other locations, and this seems reasonable given this \npattern.\n\nBy early next week, a few clouds do look to return, associated \nwith increasing atmospheric moisture as the ridge expands eastward\nand midlevel flow shifts from northerly to westerly. North of the\narea, a trough will attempt to dig into the Midwest; however, our\npersistent ridge will prevent this trough from pushing into our \narea, even as it flattens a bit. At the surface, an area of low \npressure associated with this trough will translate east into \nQuebec, with a cold front extending through the Great Lakes \nregion. However, given the upper level pattern and persistent \nsurface high pressure, this front is not expected to make it into \nKentucky by the end of the period. Model spread does increase by \nTuesday, with some disagreement regarding how quickly the ridge \nbreaks down. Even so, the most favored outcome at this time is yet\nanother pleasant fall day, with PoPs only peaking in the very low\nsingle digits.",
